n does not make the point of view unchallengeable.\n\nD) \"Among primates, fruit-eating animals have larger brains than leaf-eating animals. This is because fruits are more dispersed in time and space, and finding fruits is a more complicated task.\"\n- This option provides a different reason for larger brain sizes in primates, specifically related to the complexity of finding food. However, it does not directly challenge the given point of view that social behavior is the primary reason. Instead, it offers an alternative explanation for brain size differences. Therefore, this option does not make the point of view unchallengeable.\n\nAfter analyzing all the options, the only one that would make the point of view unchallengeable is option C, as it introduces a new factor (the cerebral cortex) that is important for primate cognition and spatial reasoning, but does not directly contradict the idea that social behavior is the primary reason for larger brain sizes.\n</think>\n<answer>C</answer>\n</Assistant>",
