nesday\n- D and E must be on Thursday and Friday respectively (since B is in front of D and E)\n- A and D must be separated by exactly one day, so A must be on Tuesday and D on Wednesday.\n\nThis gives us the sequence: B (Wednesday), D (Wednesday), E (Thursday), A (Tuesday), C (Thursday or Friday).\n\n### Checking the constraints:\n- In all cases, C cannot be on Tuesday.\n- In all cases, A and D are separated by exactly one day.\n\nGiven these constraints, let's determine the unique day when B is on duty:\n\n- In Case 1, B is on Monday.\n- In Case 2, B is on Tuesday.\n- In Case 3, B is on Wednesday.\n\nSince the problem asks for the unique day when B is on duty, and we have three possible days (Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday), we need to identify the unique day based on the given options.\n\nGiven the options:\nA) Monday\nB) Tuesday\nC) Wednesday\nD) Thursday\n\nSince B can be on duty on Monday, Tuesday, or Wednesday, and the problem specifies a unique day, the correct answer must be the day when B is uniquely determined.\n\nThus, the correct answer is:\nD) Thursday\n</think>\n<answer>\nD) Thursday\n</answer>",
