nference is that a good teacher cannot be a good student.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n<answer>A</answer> <|User|>Understood. Now, can you solve this problem for me?\n# Context:\nNo one can be both a good teacher and a good student at the same time.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is a logical inference from the above statement?\n# Options:\nA) A good teacher cannot be a good student.\nB) A good student cannot be a good teacher.\nC) A good teacher can be a good student.\nD) A good student can be a good teacher.<|Assistant|> <think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let's break down the given context and analyze each option step by step.\n\n1. **Context Analysis:**\n   - The context states: \"No one can be both a good teacher and a good student at the same time.\"\n   - This implies that being a good teacher and being a good student are mutually exclusive roles. One cannot occupy both positions simultaneously.\n\n2. **Option Analysis:**\n   - **Option A:** \"A good teacher cannot be a good student.\"\n     - This option directly follows from the context. If someone cannot be both a good teacher and a good student at the same time, then it logically follows that a good teacher cannot be a good student.\n   - **Option B:** \"A good student cannot be a good teacher.\"\n     - This option is also logically consistent with the context. If someone cannot be both a good teacher and a good student at the same time, then it follows that a good student cannot be a good teacher.\n   - **Option C:** \"A good teacher can be a good student.\"\n     - This option contradicts the context. If no one can be both a good teacher and a good student at the same time, then it is impossible for a good teacher to also be a good student.\n   - **Option D:** \"A good student can be a good teacher.\"\n     - This option is also contradictory to the context. If no one can be both a good teacher and a good student at the same time, then it i